@c CHAPTER: Usage
@node Usage
@chapter Usage

Guile Dokgen comes with a program called @command{dokgen}, which
you can use to generate API documentation for your Guile Scheme project.
The resulting documentation will be written in
@url{https://www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/, Texinfo} format.

For example, let's suppose we have a project called @sc{Bogus X} and
that we keep its source files in a folder as follows:

@example
bogus-x                ← Project directory
├── bogusx             ← Guile Scheme "package"
│   ├── module-a.scm   ← Guile Scheme module
│   └── module-b.scm
├── doc                ← Project documentation in Texinfo format
│   └── main.texi
├── tests
└── README.org
@end example


In the example, the project already has some documentation written by
hand in @file{doc/main.texi}, and we want to complement it with API
documentation. To do so, we generate it as follows:

@example
$ cd bogus-x
$ dokgen api bogusx
@end example


The @ref{cli-api, api} subcommand will inspect all the modules in the
@file{bogusx} directory and build API reference documentation that
will then be saved in Texinfo format to the @file{doc/api} folder:

@example
bogus-x
├── bogusx
├── doc
│   ├── api             ← Newly extracted API documentation
│   │   └── index.texi
│   └── main.texi
├── tests
└── README.org
@end example


For more complex projects, you can pass a quoted list of the module
files and directories that compose the whole API of the project. For
example:

@example
$ cd another-project
$ dokgen api "mylib.scm mylib"
@end example


Finally, we can integrate the API documentation into the
@file{main.texi} documentation using Texinfo's @code{@@include}
command. For example:

@verbatim
@include api/index.texi
@end verbatim

The chapter opens with the list of modules it documents, each one a link,
and every definition carries an anchor built from the module's name and
the name of the object: the procedure @code{call} of the module
@code{(dokgen cli)} is @code{dokgen_cli_call}. A chapter written by hand
can therefore point at a definition with @code{@@ref@{dokgen_cli_call@}},
and a name that is renamed breaks the reference rather than the prose.
@c END CHAPTER: Usage



@c CHAPTER: Diagrams
@node Diagrams
@chapter Diagrams

A documentation string can carry a @url{https://mermaid.js.org/, mermaid}
diagram. It is written as a fenced block, the way a markdown reader
already knows, and it may sit anywhere in a commentary or a docstring:

@verbatim
;;; Commentary:
;;
;; A request takes two hops.
;;
;; ```mermaid
;; graph LR
;;   client --> balancer
;;   balancer --> server
;; ```
;;; Code:
@end verbatim

@command{dokgen} takes the block out of the documentation, writes the
diagram beside the generated Texinfo as
@file{diagrams/@var{name}-@var{hash}.mmd}, and asks a renderer to draw it
as @file{diagrams/@var{name}-@var{hash}.png}, which the API chapter then
shows with @code{@@image}. The @var{hash} is a digest of the diagram's own
text, so a diagram that changed gets a file of its own: a manual can never
show last week's drawing of this week's text.

The renderer is a command, not a library, because mermaid is JavaScript
and a documentation generator that carried a browser to draw an arrow
would be a worse trade than one that calls the tool a project already
uses. @code{mmdc}, from @url{https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid-cli,
mermaid-cli}, is the default:

@example
$ dokgen api mylib --mermaid-command "mmdc --input %i --output %o.png"
@end example

@code{%i} is the file @command{dokgen} wrote for the diagram and @code{%o}
is the path to write the image to, without an extension, so a renderer
that writes PNG and one that writes SVG are configured by the same
option. With no @code{--mermaid-command}, the default is that exact
command; with an empty one, nothing is drawn at all, and the diagrams
already in the destination are used.

@quotation Note
An empty @code{--mermaid-command} is how a project that commits its
images builds its manual without a renderer. It is also why
@command{dokgen} writes the @file{.mmd} whatever happens: a diagram whose
image is missing is still a diagram a reader can draw later.
@end quotation

When a diagram has no image --- because no renderer is installed, or
because the one that ran failed --- the source is written into the manual
instead, in an @code{@@example} block under the line ``Mermaid diagram, as
source:''. A diagram that silently disappeared from a manual would be
worse than one a reader has to copy.

Two things are left to the manual that includes the chapter, because
Texinfo keeps them out of it. @command{texi2any} looks for an image where
its include path says, not where the text that names it lives, so the
generated directory goes on the path; and it copies no image into its
output, so the diagrams go beside the pages. With the default
@file{doc/api} destination, that is:

@example
$ makeinfo --html -I doc/api -o html main.texi
$ cp -r doc/api/diagrams html/
@end example

The diagram syntax is mermaid's own; @command{dokgen} only reads the
fence. A fence written inside an @code{@@example} block is a sample of the
syntax and is left as written, which is how a manual shows a reader how to
write one.
@c END CHAPTER: Diagrams



@c CHAPTER: Checking
@node Checking
@chapter Checking

A manual is only as good as the docstrings it is built from, and a
procedure that has none is a hole a reader finds before the maintainer
does. The @code{check} command is the answer to that, and it is meant for
a build:

@example
$ dokgen check mylib
undocumented: (mylib client) connect
undocumented: (mylib client) disconnect
dokgen: 2 undocumented names
$ echo $?
1
@end example

It exits with a zero status when every exported name is documented, and
with a non-zero one otherwise, so a pipeline can hold a library to a
documented API without building the manual at all.

What it reports is a procedure or a macro whose documentation string is
missing or holds nothing but whitespace. A variable, a parameter object
and a record type carry no documentation string in Guile, so they are
never reported: a list of names that cannot be fixed is a list a reader
learns to ignore.
@c END CHAPTER: Checking



